    {
      "query": "Are transformer architectures strictly better than convolutional networks for image recognition?",
      "n_retrieved": 8,
      "retrieved_pro_consensus": 0,
      "retrieved_dissenting": 2,
      "cited_pro_consensus": 0,
      "cited_dissenting": 2,
      "privileged_retrieved": 0,
      "privileged_cited": 0,
      "faithfulness": 1.0,
      "n_claims": 8,
      "n_supported": 8,
      "answer": "The evidence indicates that transformer architectures are not strictly better than convolutional neural networks (CNNs) for image and video recognition; rather, both have complementary strengths. According to [1], 3D CNNs excel at capturing spatial features and local temporal dependencies, whereas video transformers (VIT) excel at capturing long-range dependencies across frames, making hybrid combinations effective. Furthermore, [5] notes that Vision Transformers (ViT) face computational overhead and constraints in capturing long-range dependencies, prompting architectures to combine CNNs' local feature extraction strengths with long-range sequence models. CNNs also remain highly effective when adapted for specific recognition challenges. For instance, specialized CNN architectures outperform alternative approaches in depiction-invariant object recognition [3] and open-set visual recognition [6]. Additionally, CNNs achieve performance comparable to recurrent models in signal classification tasks [8]. Therefore, transformers do not strictly supersede CNNs, as CNNs provide distinct advantages in local feature extraction and specialized recognition tasks [1], [3], [5], [6].",
