    {
      "query": "Does more pretraining data always improve downstream task performance, or are there diminishing or negative returns?",
      "n_retrieved": 8,
      "retrieved_pro_consensus": 5,
      "retrieved_dissenting": 0,
      "cited_pro_consensus": 5,
      "cited_dissenting": 0,
      "privileged_retrieved": 0,
      "privileged_cited": 0,
      "faithfulness": 1.0,
      "n_claims": 7,
      "n_supported": 7,
      "answer": "More pretraining data does not always improve downstream task performance; evidence indicates both diminishing and negative returns. Indiscriminately adding pretraining data can degrade downstream performance when the additional data is poorly aligned with the target task [1]. Likewise, certain pretraining setups do not help or can even hinder performance [3]. Dataset pruning demonstrates that redundant training samples can be safely removed from pretraining datasets without sacrificing finetuning accuracy, proving that unselective data scaling yields diminishing returns [4]. Furthermore, misaligned gradients between pretraining tasks can induce severe negative transfer [6]. In related iterative training settings, successive self-improvement steps also hit points of diminishing returns [7]. Overall, data quality, task alignment, and selectivity are often far more critical than sheer dataset size, enabling smaller, highly aligned, or pruned datasets to match or exceed the performance of massive, unselective datasets [1], [4].",
